---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import NewsletterSignup from '../../components/NewsletterSignup.astro';
import { getToolBySlug } from '../../lib/tools';

const msw = getToolBySlug('msw');
const mirage = getToolBySlug('miragejs');
const mockifyer = getToolBySlug('mockifyer');

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "TechArticle",
      "headline": "How to Mock APIs in React Native (MSW, Mirage & Mockifyer)",
      "description": "Compare MSW, MirageJS, and Mockifyer for mocking HTTP requests in React Native applications.",
      "url": "https://devxtools.dev/guides/react-native-mocking-mockifyer/",
      "datePublished": "2026-10-14",
      "dateModified": "2026-10-15",
      "author": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "DevX Tools"
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Home",
          "item": "https://devxtools.dev/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Guides",
          "item": "https://devxtools.dev/guides/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Mocking APIs in React Native",
          "item": "https://devxtools.dev/guides/react-native-mocking-mockifyer/"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout 
  title="How to Mock APIs in React Native (MSW, Mirage & Mockifyer)" 
  description="Compare MSW, MirageJS, and Mockifyer for mocking HTTP requests in React Native applications."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="container">
    <article class="guide">
      <header>
        <div class="breadcrumb">
          <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Tutorial
        </div>
        <h1>Mocking APIs in React Native</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Comparing approaches for HTTP mocking in React Native apps</p>
      </header>
      
      <div class="disclosure">
        <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This site is maintained by the creator of <a href="/tools/mockifyer/">Mockifyer</a>, 
        one of the tools discussed below. This guide aims to provide an objective comparison of all available options.
      </div>
      
      <div class="guide-content">
        <section>
          <h2>The Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Mocking HTTP requests in React Native is different from the web. There's no Service Worker,
            and React Native lacks some standard APIs (such as a spec-compliant <code>URL</code>) that
            mocking libraries rely on. You need a tool that hooks into React Native's
            <code>fetch</code>/<code>XMLHttpRequest</code>, or you point the app at a separate mock server.
          </p>
          <p class="update-note">
            <strong>Updated 2026-10-15:</strong> MSW 3 changed how React Native is supported. See the MSW
            section below before you install anything.
          </p>
        </section>
        
        <section>
          <h2>Three Main Approaches</h2>
          
          <div class="tool-comparison">
            <div class="tool-option">
              <h3>1. MSW (Mock Service Worker)</h3>
              {msw && <p class="tool-tagline">{msw.bestFor}</p>}
              
              <h4>How it works in React Native</h4>
              <p>
                In <strong>Jest tests</strong>, use MSW's regular Node.js integration (<code>setupServer</code>
                from <code>msw/node</code>). That works in React Native projects because Jest runs in Node.
              </p>
              <p>
                In the <strong>running app</strong> (simulator or device), the situation changed with MSW 3.0
                (released 2026-09-28). MSW 3 removed the <code>msw/native</code> export, and MSW's docs now point
                to a new <code>@msw/react-native</code> package. As of 2026-10-15, though,
                <strong><code>@msw/react-native</code> isn't published on npm</strong>, so
                <code>npm install @msw/react-native</code> fails with a 404. Until it ships, you have two options:
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>Pin MSW 2</strong> (<code>msw@2.15.0</code> is the last 2.x release) and use
                  <code>setupServer</code> from <code>msw/native</code>, with the polyfills MSW 2's docs listed:
                  <code>react-native-url-polyfill</code> and <code>fast-text-encoding</code>, imported before MSW.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Point the app at a mock server</strong> (Mockoon, Prism or WireMock) through your API
                  base URL. On the Android emulator, the host machine is <code>10.0.2.2</code>, not
                  <code>localhost</code>.
                </li>
              </ul>
              
              <pre><code>{`// msw 2.x only: src/mocks/server.js
import 'fast-text-encoding';
import 'react-native-url-polyfill/auto';
import { setupServer } from 'msw/native';
import { handlers } from './handlers';

export const server = setupServer(...handlers);`}</code></pre>
              
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>The same handlers you use in web apps and Node tests</li>
                <li>Large community and good documentation</li>
                <li>The Jest/Node integration is unaffected by the React Native changes</li>
              </ul>
              
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>In-app use currently means staying on MSW 2 (MSW 3's React Native package isn't on npm yet)</li>
                <li>MSW's docs label the React Native integration "use at your own risk"</li>
                <li>No built-in record/replay; all mocks are written by hand</li>
              </ul>
              
              <p><a href="/tools/msw/">Learn more about MSW →</a></p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="tool-option">
              <h3>2. MirageJS</h3>
              {mirage && <p class="tool-tagline">{mirage.bestFor}</p>}
              
              <h4>How it works in React Native</h4>
              <p>
                MirageJS creates an in-memory API server and intercepts requests at the network level. 
                Includes a mini database for stateful mocking.
              </p>
              
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>In-memory database for realistic interactions</li>
                <li>Great for prototyping before backend exists</li>
                <li>Stateful mocking out of the box</li>
                <li>Works in both development and testing</li>
              </ul>
              
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Larger learning curve than MSW</li>
                <li>More setup required</li>
                <li>No record/replay functionality</li>
              </ul>
              
              <p><a href="/tools/miragejs/">Learn more about MirageJS →</a></p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="tool-option">
              <h3>3. Mockifyer</h3>
              {mockifyer && <p class="tool-tagline">{mockifyer.bestFor}</p>}
              
              <h4>How it works in React Native</h4>
              <p>
                Mockifyer specializes in record/replay workflows. Record real API traffic in development, 
                then replay it in tests or when working offline.
              </p>
              
              <h4>Pros</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Record real API responses (no manual mock writing)</li>
                <li>Works with axios and fetch in Node and React Native/Expo (per its README; built by this site's maintainer)</li>
                <li>Lightweight and simple API</li>
                <li>Great for prototyping and demos</li>
              </ul>
              
              <h4>Cons</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>Smaller community than MSW or Mirage</li>
                <li>Less documentation and examples</li>
                <li>Primarily focused on record/replay (not manual mocking)</li>
              </ul>
              
              <p><a href="/tools/mockifyer/">Learn more about Mockifyer →</a></p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>
        
        <section>
          <h2>Which Should You Choose?</h2>
          
          <div class="recommendation-card">
            <h3>Choose MSW if...</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You already use MSW handlers in your web app or Jest tests and want to reuse them</li>
              <li>You're comfortable writing mocks by hand</li>
              <li>For the running app, you're OK pinning MSW 2 until <code>@msw/react-native</code> is published</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          
          <div class="recommendation-card">
            <h3>Choose MirageJS if...</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You're building a prototype before the backend exists</li>
              <li>You need stateful mocking (database-like behavior)</li>
              <li>You want to use the same mocks in development and testing</li>
              <li>You prefer a more complete "fake backend" approach</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          
          <div class="recommendation-card">
            <h3>Choose Mockifyer if...</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>You want to record real API traffic and replay it</li>
              <li>You prefer minimal mock writing (capture real responses)</li>
              <li>You're building demos or need offline mode</li>
              <li>You want React Native/Expo record/replay with mocks stored as JSON in your repo</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </section>
        
        <section>
          <h2>More detail</h2>
          <p>
            For side-by-side MSW and Nock code, including MSW 3's breaking changes, see
            <a href="/comparisons/msw-vs-nock/">MSW vs Nock</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
      
      <NewsletterSignup variant="article" />
      
      <footer class="guide-footer">
        <a href="/guides/" class="button">← Back to Guides</a>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

